PUBLIC RESPONSE: {A,D

The demographic data are unambiguous: the 18-year-old cohort fell from ~2.05 M in the early 1990s to ~1.1 M today, tracking 1990s birth projections almost exactly, so A’s premise that the drop was “smaller than predicted” is simply false and cannot explain the rise in entrants. D is likewise mismatched with observed institutional trends: junior-college and senmon-gakkō enrollments have contracted sharply, and the handful of new Professional and Vocational Universities (post-2019) operate as small parallel tracks rather than systematic feeders that inflate conventional four-year head-counts.

B and E remain the dominant, well-documented offsets—enrollment rates among new graduates roughly doubling and decades of deregulation plus subsidies enabling the 1.6-fold growth in institutions. C, while directionally plausible in graduate and non-degree programs, adds only a marginal increment to the headline undergraduate entrant statistics that drive the overall trend; treating it as a primary explanation therefore overstates its scale. The two options that are either empirically false or conceptually unsuitable are therefore A and D.}

